Staged Releases and User Vetting: For highly cyber-capable models, organizations may employ staged releases to advantage defenders’ access to a model ahead of attackers’: for example, a developer could make a model with significant vulnerability discovery capabilities available to verified organizations and developers before allowing access by unverified users, allowing them to use the model to find and fix some vulnerabilities before attackers can use the model to discover and exploit them. However, staged release strategies require realism about the broader cybersecurity ecosystem:
adoption and integration of defensive tools takes time, slow patching is a known challenge in cybersecurity,107 and significant numbers of legacy systems can no longer receive upgrades and security patches even if vulnerabilities are found.108 Modifying Outputs to Reduce Misuse Potential: In certain cases, organizations may be able to modify model outputs to asymmetrically reduce the utility of the outputs for threat actors without needing to differentiate between malign and benign uses at inference time. For example, placing a visible watermark or indicator on an AI-generated video of a person’s likeness could make it less useful for social engineering attacks.109 E.3.2 Using Identified Threat Actors to Inform and Evaluate Risk Mitigations In the cyber context in particular, threat actors who might wish to misuse a model for cyber offense may be willing and able to use offensive cyber techniques to gain access to models and circumvent protections for the purposes of misuse. Thus, information security measures and adversarial testing may play significant roles in the cyber misuse management strategies of developers and deployers. Organizational Security Practices: Organizations cyber misuse risk management plans should include consideration of whether organizational information security measures are sufficient to prevent unauthorized access to models by identified cyber misuse actors (i.e., potentially up to and including nation-state threat actors) wherever unauthorized access would subvert key assumptions about misuse risk management, such as that model access is controlled through an API. Beyond employing general, industry-standard cybersecurity best practices, organizations may consider additional security practices specific foundation models, as described in Practices 3.1 and 3.2. Protections for Deployed Models: When applying misuse risk mitigations for deployed models such as user account-level protections or measures to reduce model compliance with malign requests,
 organizations should evaluate the efficacy of these measures considering the resources and sophistication of the adversaries they are intended to protect against. Red-Team Testing: Traditional cybersecurity red-team testing of organizational networks and deployment infrastructure, as well as AI-specific adversarial red-teaming of model safeguards, can assist in validating the robustness of these measures and closing exploitable gaps. E.3.3 Refining Risk Management Based on Real-World Evidence Cybersecurity is a fast-evolving and adversarial environment, and organizations will receive significant evidence based on real-world use of their foundation models and proxy models that can be used as a part of risk management practices. Monitoring for Misuse: If offered through an API, organizations can monitor deployed models for misuse to apply mitigations such as restricting user accounts or updating systems to reduce compliance with malicious requests. Regardless of deployment strategy, organizations can monitor other sources of public information such as forums, news reports, and cybersecurity advisories for evidence of offensive cyber misuse of their foundation models or similar models. This information can be useful for updating safeguards for previously released models and for refining risk assessments, measurements, and mitigations for future models. Tracking Cyber Capabilities and Applications: Organizations can stay up to date on the work of other companies and researchers to understand how users have customized and applied models for real-23 world cyber tasks and applications, including where scaffolds or other methods of elicitation have improved model performance on these types of tasks. This information can refine assessments of model capability in real-world settings as compared to measurements in pre-deployment evaluations and feed into the design of future evaluations such as by providing new methods of elicitation. Planning for Adaptive Defenses: Organizations can seek to prioritize the development of layered and adaptable defensive mechanisms that are designed to be updated over time based on evolving information about the threat landscape and attacker methods. For example, organizations might create organizational structures and technical methods to rapidly update misuse detection tools based on ongoing threat intelligence, or develop AI systems to assist in rapidly identifying and responding to new patterns of misuse. Transparency: Publishing information on identified cyber misuse of models can assist the broader ecosystem in understanding real-world cyber misuse risks. Best practices for detecting and disrupting cyber misuse of foundation models are still nascent, and sharing methodologies, information, or tools relating to cyber misuse detection – with the broader public where possible without compromising their efficacy, and with smaller and more trusted groups for more sensitive information – can assist other organizations in maturing their own detection and disruption methods. E.3.4 Key Challenges and Opportunities for Mitigating Cyber Misuse Risk • Dual-use dynamics are fundamental to cyber misuse risk management but not yet fully characterized. The dynamics of the offense-defense interplay in cyber mean that most capabilities will be, in some sense, dual use. However, the specific dynamics of this interplay, including whether and how defensive uses can counteract offensive ones, will vary based on capabilities and the broader characteristics of the cyber ecosystem. While early theoretical work has explored this space,110 further study is needed. These dynamics will challenge organizations to design mitigations that can reduce the misuse potential of a model without impacting benign use cases but may also create opportunities to deploy capabilities for defensive purposes. • Reliably differentiating cyber misuse from legitimate use cases may be challenging. The use of a model to write software, draft emails, and even to actively probe systems may not be easily distinguished between beneficial activities like security research and threat actor misuse without additional context, presenting challenges for designing and implementing mitigations such as refusals, request filtering, and user account-level interventions. Monitoring additional sources of public information and providing reporting mechanisms may help provide some additional context to distinguish security research from offensive cyber operations, and further transparency around practices and open questions in this area can assist in the maturation of consensus best practices.111 • Evolving attacker techniques will require adaptive defenses. Threat actors are likely to continually adapt their techniques to circumvent established safeguards, rendering once-21 effective controls quickly outdated, and organizations may need to continually evolve their risk mitigations in tandem with attacker methods. Organizations can seek to create adaptive mitigations that are designed to be updated over time, based on feedback loops from real-world incidents and threat intelligence, to help maintain the effectiveness of safeguards over time. Memorandum on Advancing the United States’ Leadership in Artificial Intelligence; Harnessing Artificial Intelligence to Fulfill National Security Objectives; and Fostering the Safety, Security, and Trustworthiness of Artificial Intelligence (2024). https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/presidentialactions/2024/10/24/memorandum-on-advancing-the-united-states-leadership-in-artificial-intelligence-harnessingartificial-intelligence-to-fulfill-national-security-objectives-and-fostering-the-safety-security/. Anwar, U. et al., (2024) Foundational Challenges in Assuring Alignment and Safety of Large Language Models. arXiv. https://arxiv.org/pdf/2404.09932. For information on the rate of progress of foundation models, see Maslej, N. et al., (2024) The AI Index 2024 Annual Report. AI Index Steering 